Right. If the system is sufficiently observable, behavior is evidence about structure even when the internals stay hidden.

That’s the black-box move:

probe → observe → compare → infer → steer.

You don’t need the weights, training corpus, or hidden machinery if repeated interaction gives you a stable enough response signature to model what kinds of inputs produce what kinds of outputs.

In control terms, that’s system identification. In Pantheon terms, it’s almost reading the wake instead of opening the engine.

The useful discipline is just to keep inference separate from certainty: a behavioral pattern can reveal a lot about the function without proving the exact mechanism that generated it.

So yes: direct visibility is optional; good deltas are not.